that *"q and nu are estimable from the archive"* with no check behind it, which is false on both counts and is the sentence that tells him what a paid seat buys.** **WHAT THIS CHANGES ABOUT THE DECISION.** It is no longer *add 1 field for the difficulty estimator*. It is **whether to record the class of missing reference points — 4 fields across 3 writers — or only the 1 this entry began with.** The class costs little more than the instance and makes 3 further quantities measurable; the instance is narrower and leaves the other 3 where they are. Both remain his. Producer: `scripts/the_archive_records_outcomes_not_reference_points_2026-11-02.py`, which runs read-only, prints every interval on 2 tools, and carries `--sizing` for the floor table. ★ **INSTANCE 4 IS CORRECTED 2026-11-02T20:26:35+00:00, AND THE CORRECTION MAKES THIS ENTRY'S CLAIM STRONGER RATHER THAN WEAKER.** The compaction-boundary rule required re-running every producer behind the preceding commit, because its figures were produced from a summary rather than from the project. **1 figure of roughly 12 moved: "0 producers estimate d or p" became 3, and chasing it reversed the claim 3 times.** **(1) "q HAS NO ESTIMATOR AT ALL" IS FALSE.** `bench/canary_seeding.py` defines `detection_rate`, whose own docstring reads *"p_hat = catches / k per model, over HELD-OUT canaries only"*, with held-out splits *"because a canary the panel has already been scored on is a canary it can have learned"*. That is exactly q's DETECTION factor, by mutation testing pointed at reviewers rather than at a test suite, built on **his ruling of 2026-08-27**: *"Build it, test it, implement it in its own independent 3 model panel review ... give it its own panel/build process."* It is guarded by `bench/tests/test_canary_seeding_2026-08-28.py` and called from the runner. **(2) "IT APPEARS NEVER TO HAVE BEEN RUN" IS ALSO FALSE.** The archive holds 3: `bench/logs/canary_build_panel_2026-08-28`, `bench/logs/sim45_canary_20260901T145630Z_20260901T145632Z` and `bench/logs/sim45_canary_v2_20260901T214242Z_20260901T214244Z`. **(3) SO THE REAL FINDING IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS: those runs' files mention `p_hat` and `catches` and record 0 STRUCTURED VALUES, so no measured detection rate can be read out of them.** **THIS IS A 4TH CASE OF THIS ENTRY'S PATTERN AND THE STRONGEST OF THEM, NOT AN EXCEPTION TO IT.** The other 3 lack a recorded FIELD. This one lacks the recorded OUTPUT of a mechanism that exists, is guarded, and has been run 3 times — the work was done and discarded, which is the same shape as the discrimination control at B83 computing an outcome 48 times a run and storing none of it. **SO ALL 4 ARE BLOCKED BY SOMETHING COMPUTED AND NOT KEPT, which is a tighter statement than the one this entry first made.** **AND q's PRECISION FACTOR STILL HAS NO ESTIMATOR:** `canary_seeding` supplies 1 of the 2 factors, and the "precision 1.000" mentions in that module concern the answer-key leak hazard rather than model precision. **2 DEFECTS IN THE PRODUCER ARE FIXED IN THE SAME PASS:** it matched its OWN search pattern, the 5th instance of that trap on 2026-11-02, now excluded by identity with the pattern assembled from parts so it cannot spell itself; and its verdict line asserted an absence it had not established, now printing each match with a judgement rather than a bare count. ★ **FIELD 1 OF 4 IS BUILT, 2026-11-08, AND THE OTHER 3 ARE NOT — WHICH IS WHY THE ASK IS NARROWED RATHER THAN RETIRED.** `pre_run_target_description` in `bench/reference_runner_v3.py` writes `pre_run_target` into the checkpoint: the target's path, kind, byte size, line count and sha256, captured on the FIRST checkpoint of a run and never overwritten, because `_apply_back_gate` can fold a fix back into the working copy and a later reading would store a post-fix target under a pre-run name. Guard: `bench/tests/test_the_run_state_records_its_target_2026-11-08.py`, 6 tests, including the cache property and a named-failure path so an unreadable target cannot report a size of 0 that would read as a measurement. It costs 1 file read on 1 checkpoint, so it spends nothing on his machine. **WHAT EACH OF THE REMAINING 3 NEEDS, AND NONE OF IT IS A FIELD.** The BASELINE PASS RATE needs an extra suite execution: `_run_effect_regression` runs with a 120-second timeout and 2,333 archived entries carry a proposed fix, so measuring it per fix is unaffordable — but the baseline is a property of the TARGET and the suite rather than of a fix, so computing it ONCE per run and caching it costs 1 extra suite run, and that execution budget is his to release. `W(class)` needs a SOURCE: section 11 of the operational directive instructs every model to classify constraints HARD or SOFT before producing output, and no field anywhere receives that classification, so the value would have to be parsed from a seat's response. `confidence` needs a source too: B87's narrowing of 2026-11-08 made a tool-derived confidence ADMISSIBLE, which is not the same as available. **[NEEDS HIS RULING] on the 1 extra suite run per run, and on where the HARD/SOFT class and the confidence are to come from.** ★ **STILL OPEN AS OF 2026-11-10, AND THE REASON IT IS RECORDED AGAIN IS THAT THE REPORT GENERATOR WAS NOT ASKING.** This entry carried a live `[NEEDS HIS RULING]` and his ruling of 2026-11-08 beside it, so the generator classified it as settled work and printed it in **0** of the spoken reports — found by `bench/tests/test_the_two_decision_surfaces_cannot_diverge_2026-11-01.py`, which reported it as carrying an authored heading and being rendered nowhere. His ruling settled the ESTIMATOR; it did not answer what arose from it. **WHAT IS STILL OWED, UNCHANGED AND RESTATED HERE SO IT IS ASKED:** the 1 extra suite run per run, which is an execution budget on his 8 cores and therefore his to release; and where the HARD/SOFT constraint class and the confidence are to come from, since section 11 of the operational directive tells every model to c