er manager`, **48 direct string comparisons** of the form `mc.role == "collator"` or `mc.role != "player_manager"`, **29 `role=` keyword arguments** carrying the old spellings, and **79 `Role.PM` / `Role.COL` / `Role.PAR` references** across 7 files, 4 of them under `bench/dm/`. **THE SILENT-FAILURE MODE, DEMONSTRATED BY EXECUTION RATHER THAN ARGUED.** An alias table cannot rescue a direct comparison. With his vocabulary added to `ROLE_ALIASES` in memory, `canonical_role` accepts all 3 new names and maps them to the declared members. But a `ModelConfig` whose stored role is `"classifier"` makes `mc.role == "collator"` evaluate **False** while `canonical_role(mc.role) == "collator"` evaluates **True**, and no exception is raised in either case. So flipping which spelling is canonical, without rewriting each of the 48 predicates, turns a True into a False in silence -- which is the defect class this project's record is mostly made of. **THE 3 OPTIONS, AND THE CHOICE IS HIS.** **(1) ALIASES ONLY:** enter `orchestrator`, `classifier` and `worker` into `ROLE_ALIASES` pointing at the current declared members. His vocabulary becomes valid input everywhere `canonical_role` runs; 3 lines change; 0 stored values and 0 predicates change; nothing can break. The cost is that the canonical spellings, and therefore 48 predicates and the enum comments, still say collator. **(2) FLIP CANONICAL AND ROUTE EVERY PREDICATE THROUGH `canonical_role`:** his words become the real names, the old 3 become legacy aliases exactly as `ds` and `dis` were retained pointing flat at `disc`, and all 48 comparison sites plus 29 construction sites are rewritten. This is the option that actually carries his ruling into the code. Its risk is a missed site, and a missed site is silent. **(3) FLIP CANONICAL AND LEAVE THE PREDICATES:** named only to be refused, because it is exactly the silent-False case above. **THE RECOMMENDATION, under the 3 formal standards.** Option 1 now and option 2 as a scoped follo